                      <exercise>




      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson. Double click on mathematical
  expressions/equations to enlarge. If needed, use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
      <!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->

        <exercise id="_54828607-798d-48f4-9098-8816f83d3f26" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Whenever the input of a function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 1, the output increases by 5. Which of these equations
          could define <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00004"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00005"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00006"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para><para id="para-00007"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></para></problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00010">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_4c75059f-e45f-4203-9d6d-2787a3c8b931" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00011">The function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Which of the following statements is true about the values of
          <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?<emphasis>Select all</emphasis> that apply.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">The correct answer is b and d.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_968c16ab-3a12-494b-9d43-4b32752c1cab" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00015">The two lines on the coordinate plane are graphs of functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Use for the graph below for
        questions 3 and 4.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">
              
                <media alt="Graph of 2 lines.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_17_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00017">Use the graph to explain why the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 2 each time the input <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by
          1. 
      </para>
            <para id="para-00018">Multiple Choice:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00019">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> are two points on the graph of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Between the two points,
        <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 2 and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 1.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a146c478-5f35-4970-b07d-8d9594463256" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00022">Use the graph to explain why the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 2 each time the input <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> increases by 1.
         <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> that apply.
      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2.</para>
            <para id="para-00025">The correct answer is a and d.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1107dbf8-66fc-45a5-9745-97e5975d3473" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00026">The function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Find the quotient <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. 
      </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00029">The correct answer is 25.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_5674259f-b084-4426-bbff-5cf458fbcbd1" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00030">The function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Find the quotient <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00031">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00032">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.3.</para>
            <para id="para-00033">The correct answer is 125.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_68556bd3-af50-4ef9-b72b-3b62d0a83449" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00034">For each of the functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>q</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the domain is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>≤</m:mi><m:mn>100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. For which functions is
          the average rate of change a good measure of how the function changes for this domain?<emphasis>Select three</emphasis> that
          apply.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00035">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00036">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.4. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00037">The correct answer is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>h</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>111</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>23</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>q</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>87.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2ba83d50-cf33-400c-a317-9a186fa46fc3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00038">What would be the rate of change of the following table?</para>
            <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00001">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
             </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
             </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                1
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                -4
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                -2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                3
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                0
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                5
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00039">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00040">Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.
    </para>
            <para id="para-00041">The correct answer is 2.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_9c739c4f-ee63-4f46-bfdd-8ccfeb05fbc7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00042">Given the following table, what would be <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <table class="unnumbered os-raise-skinnytable" id="table-00002">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>
             </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
                <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
             </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                1
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                2
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                3
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                8
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                4
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                16
              </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                5
              </entry>
                  <entry>
                32
              </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00043">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00044">Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
    </para>
            <para id="para-00045">The correct answer is 2.
    </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bf41e467-06b4-41c1-8496-accddf0ee0a0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00046">Which of the following statements compare rates of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:msup><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00048">In <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00049">In <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you add 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">In <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you add 3 to get to the next term; in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
            <para id="para-00051">In <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you add 3 to get to the next term; in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00052">Correct!</para>
            <para id="para-00053">Try Again!</para>
            <para id="para-00054">You seem to be struggling. Who can you ask for help?</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content></document>